  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic summary of how CHDP, a host defense peptide, may influence immunity and tissue repair in a wound. At the center, CHDP is shown as a regulator with many outward effects rather than a single pathway. It appears to act directly against microbes and to modulate the microbiome, while also neutralizing LPS from gram-negative bacteria. Beyond antimicrobial activity, the peptide reshapes the inflammatory environment by increasing anti-inflammatory cytokines and suppressing pro-inflammatory cytokines, and it alters endotoxin-induced cellular signaling.\n\nThe diagram also emphasizes immune-cell recruitment and activation. CHDP induces chemokines, which seem to attract monocytes/macrophages, and it is linked to enhanced phagocytosis, differentiation of dendritic cells, and recruitment and polarization of T cells. Another branch suggests promotion of NET formation. At the same time, CHDP enhances pro-inflammatory responses to DNA/RNA, indicating that its effects can be context dependent rather than simply suppressive.\n\nOverall, the figure presents CHDP as a multifunctional mediator that bridges antimicrobial defense, innate and adaptive immune modulation, and wound healing, coordinating both pathogen control and the host repair response.",
